WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
﻿"If you haven't seen Verdun, you haven't seen&nbsp;
anything." So wrote a French soldier from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.380
front in 1916. It was in fact just outside&nbsp;
Verdun that one of the bloodiest battles in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.480
human history and one of the decisive acts&nbsp;
of World War I was fought. A carnage said by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:21.240
many to be unnecessary. The French believed that&nbsp;
the network of fortifications around Verdun was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.460
invincible and unconquerable and had deployed much&nbsp;
of their artillery elsewhere, while the Germans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.440
intended to take advantage of this mistake and&nbsp;
force the enemy to concentrate the bulk of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.660
troops here and bleed them dry under the blows&nbsp;
of their own artillery: more than a thousand guns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:42.300
of various calibers including the fearsome "Big&nbsp;
Bertha," supplied by 2 million shells. Verdun was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.100
of vital importance to the French and they would&nbsp;
defend it at great cost. It is believed that every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.740
French soldier employed in World War I fought,&nbsp;
for at least one day, at Verdun. Bad tactics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:58.140
intensive bombing and the use of gas weapons did&nbsp;
the rest. Even today, we still don't know for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.720
sure how many soldiers took part in the fighting&nbsp;
that saw the French and German armies face each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:10.200
other for three hundred days, from February 21 to&nbsp;
December 19, 1916. Not to mention the casualties:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.480
between injured, dead, and missing, the number&nbsp;
could easily reach a million. As would later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:21.060
happen in Stalingrad, Verdun became a symbol of&nbsp;
resistance and heroism, but also of suffering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.940
death and madness One had to have passed through&nbsp;
Verdun to truly understand the horrors of war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:31.860
argued the unknown French soldier, and Verdun&nbsp;
still has much to teach us today. If we want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.660
get a taste of what the world might be like at&nbsp;
the end of a world conflict always just around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:42.060
the corner, a trip there can give us some answers,&nbsp;
even one hundred and six years later. At the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:47.340
of the war, despite the victory and triumph, a&nbsp;
real scar remained on that slice of northeastern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:52.500
France: the bombing had destroyed villages&nbsp;
and devastated the landscape, many unexploded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.720
bombs still undermined the land, and somewhere&nbsp;
underground there must still have been bunkers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.840
loaded with weapons, gases and chemical compounds.&nbsp;
And among those fields and forests still lay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.240
buried the remains of untold numbers of fallen&nbsp;
men and animals, in quantities and conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:14.280
that made all recovery and burial impossible. The&nbsp;
French government, in the aftermath of the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:19.680
of the war, merely isolated some one thousand two&nbsp;
hundred square kilometers of death and destruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.020
and proceeded with the first superficial&nbsp;
reclamation works, hoping that nature would take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:30.720
its course. The few residents who remained after&nbsp;
the war were moved out, and all agricultural use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.600
was prohibited on that land. Villages still intact&nbsp;
were abandoned and those destroyed by bombing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:42.000
such as Douamont, never rebuilt. It was clear&nbsp;
that the total reclamation work would take years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.240
if not centuries. It was called Zone rouge,&nbsp;
red zone: a non-contiguous wasteland extended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.980
between Lille and Nancy, passing, of course,&nbsp;
through Verdun. The Zone rouge is in other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:59.280
words a land poisoned by the chemical waste of&nbsp;
unexploded ordnance, by lead, mercury and arsenic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.580
by the toxic refluxes released by animal càrcasses&nbsp;
and buried human remains. The Zone rouge basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:10.260
corresponds to what was the front line, that&nbsp;
is, the area where the fighting and with it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:15.360
the destruction was total. All life is all but&nbsp;
denied in this zone. Vegetation doesn't grow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.300
and if a human being even managed to escape the&nbsp;
detonation of some unexploded ordnance he or she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:26.580
would be immediately infected by a lethal mix of&nbsp;
chemical and toxic agents. Think that the arsenic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:31.620
levels recorded within this area are around one&nbsp;
hundred seventynine milligrams per kilogram,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:38.160
when in Italy the maximum allowable limit is just&nbsp;
twenty milligrams. However, the French government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.560
has carried out remediation works over the&nbsp;
years, at least in the least compromised areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:49.680
so much so that as of today the zone rouge is&nbsp;
restricted to only one hundred square kilometers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.420
Some areas are no longer off-limits,&nbsp;
but according to experts, at this rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:59.760
it will still take seven hundred years to fully&nbsp;
secure the area. On the other hand, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.920
the gradual reclamation of land considered&nbsp;
now cleared and handed back too quickly to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:10.380
the population has ended up exposing farmers&nbsp;
and entire communities to the slow action of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:15.600
remaining chemicals, this is because the cleanup&nbsp;
work was often conducted rushed and superficial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:21.180
Nature has not helped as recent surveys have&nbsp;
found even higher amounts of arsenic in the soil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:26.640
than those recorded in the past. Despite this,&nbsp;
farming and hunting have returned to the recovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.440
territories. The French government and the&nbsp;
European Union monitor products from the region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:36.960
but it’s clear that stricter controls would cause&nbsp;
damage to the local economy already suffering from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:42.660
a century of living with the poisons of war. Of&nbsp;
course, man's reckless actions have also added to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:48.240
the situation. In fact, one of the most dangerous&nbsp;
and "poisoned" areas of the zone rouge is located&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:54.660
north of Verdun and is known today as the "place à&nbsp;
gaz," the gas place. This is where, in the 1920s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.760
Department du Deminage officers detonated&nbsp;
any unexploded ordnance they could locate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:05.580
In doing so, however, they made the situation&nbsp;
worse, because these detonations in turn released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.320
their own toxic charges into the ground. Just&nbsp;
imagine the effects that the mass detonation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:16.080
thousands of ordnance and munitions may have had&nbsp;
on a once verdant, wooded landscape. Today, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:22.020
place à gaz looks like a piece of tundra in the&nbsp;
middle of the forests of northeastern France. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:29.100
soil is now so polluted with ammonium perchlorate&nbsp;
and arsenic, whose concentration is 1000 to 10000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:34.800
times higher than natural levels, that only a few&nbsp;
lichens and mushrooms manage to grow in this area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:40.800
What's more, only since 2004 has a scientific&nbsp;
study highlighted the danger of the area. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:46.560
it was found that the surface layers of this soil,&nbsp;
for at least 20 centimeters, consist only of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.760
burning debris. The analyses then found large&nbsp;
traces of Copper (16 mg/kg), Lead (26 mg/kg),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:55.500
Arsenic (175 mg/kg) and Zinc (133 mg/kg). The&nbsp;
place à gaz was only fenced off in 2005, and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:02.280
2012 entry was formally banned. But the zone rouge&nbsp;
is only the innermost and most dangerous part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:07.860
the areas involved in World War I fighting. Even&nbsp;
larger is in fact the so-called yellow zone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:12.600
an area that is certainly less dangerous but&nbsp;
still has a number of unexploded ordnance:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:18.360
an estimated 900 tons of ammunition and bombs&nbsp;
are recovered each year by French and Belgian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:23.880
authorities. And not only by them, because it also&nbsp;
happens in fact that these devices are found by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:29.160
farmers at work in the fields. In those parts&nbsp;
they call it récolte de fer, the iron harvest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:34.080
The problem is that under pressure from local&nbsp;
communities, a clear distinction between yellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:40.140
and red zones wasn't often made, and as a result,&nbsp;
as we said, some contaminated land was restored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:46.080
to its agricultural use too soon. In short, some&nbsp;
stretches of yellow zone may have red-zone levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:51.420
of pollution and hazard, and when abnormal&nbsp;
concentrations of toxic substances turn up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:57.480
authorities always remain vague about the possible&nbsp;
causes. And this often happens even beyond the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:01.260
boundaries of red or yellow zones. It happened,&nbsp;
for example, that in 2012 in 500 municipalities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:07.260
in the Nord and Pas-De-Calais departments, that&nbsp;
is, those regions located further north of Verdun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.700
along the Belgian border, drinking water&nbsp;
was banned because of high concentrations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:16.920
ammonium perchlorate. No one has ever provided an&nbsp;
explanation, but the location of the contaminated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:22.560
aquifers corresponds to the places where the most&nbsp;
intense fighting took place during the war, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:28.080
leaves little doubt that these municipalities far&nbsp;
from Verdun also suffer from the same problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.880
even though they don't fall into any red&nbsp;
or sometimes even yellow zones. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:38.100
it wasn't only in Verdun that fighting took&nbsp;
place. In Metz, farther east of Verdun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:43.200
the local "deminage" team in charge of clearing&nbsp;
the territories of three departments belonging to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:49.440
the old front line registers some 900 to 1,000&nbsp;
requests for action a year, with a total of 45&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:54.960
to 60 tons of ammunition collected. As long as&nbsp;
they are conventional ordnance, all it would take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:00.480
is a little caution and a safe place to detonate&nbsp;
them. But about 2 percent of those collected turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:06.840
out to be gas weapons loaded with mustard gas&nbsp;
and phosgene. Until 1997, such armaments were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:11.940
collected and stored at a specialized site, at&nbsp;
Suippes, in the Marne department, not far from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:16.380
Verdun. Given what had happened to the place à&nbsp;
gaz, it was thus preferred not to detonate them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:21.120
at least for the time being. But when France&nbsp;
signed the convention prohibiting the possession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.040
and use of chemical weapons, those weapons&nbsp;
had to be dismantled somehow. Thus the SECOIA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:27.300
project, "site d'élimination des chargements&nbsp;
d'objets identifiés anciens," was initiated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:32.340
The dismantling site was established at the&nbsp;
Mailly-le-Camp military base, where the ordnance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:38.040
would be detonated in special chambers starting&nbsp;
in 2016, marking the beginning of the toxic waste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:43.860
treatment program for an expected duration of 30&nbsp;
years. What happened in and around Verdun and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:49.200
heavy legacy that has fallen on generations a&nbsp;
century after the battle is just one emblematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:54.420
and tragic case, in numbers, of a certainly more&nbsp;
widespread problem that is often ignored. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:59.640
the war, in fact, almost all belligerent&nbsp;
countries hid and stored unused armaments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:04.860
including chemical weapons. The problem is that&nbsp;
many of these locations are unknown, whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:10.440
they are top secret locations or long forgotten&nbsp;
secrets. Staying in France, for example, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:16.140
discovered that thousands of tons of armaments and&nbsp;
munitions were stored in a submerged chamber under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:21.000
the waters of Lake d'Avrillé, or in the caves&nbsp;
of Jardel, used as a veritable dumping ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:26.700
And the more years passed, the more fragile the&nbsp;
casings that retained the toxic charges became.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:31.980
Today in the parts of Verdun everything tells&nbsp;
of the war. From memorials and charnel houses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:36.420
such as the one in Douamont, to the private&nbsp;
collections of farmers who have recovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:41.760
ammunition and relics from their fields over the&nbsp;
years. Many activities revolve around the macabre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:47.520
remembrance of the war; tours are organized that&nbsp;
come dangerously close to the red zone, maybe even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:52.740
crossing it. Trenches are reconstructed for the&nbsp;
delight of tourists. Unfortunately, it is when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.720
we think we have left the nightmares behind that&nbsp;
we should pay more attention, and in Verdun the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:03.300
horror of the war is still embedded in the ground.&nbsp;
The zone rouge in some parts fools us under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:08.100
appearance of a lush forest, albeit a poisoned&nbsp;
one. In other parts, such as the place à gaz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:13.560
it seems instead to have come straight out of the&nbsp;
pages of Thomas Eliot's Wasteland, the poem of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:19.140
post-war fall of man and his land: a barren&nbsp;
wasteland where life no longer grows, camped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:24.180
with dead trees, where not even the many buried&nbsp;
corpses have been able to sprout. Well also today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:29.220
we have finished our story. Thank you all for your&nbsp;
attention and see you in the next video. Ciao!

